.TH MOUSE 3
.SH NAME
mouse \- the pointer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #c /dev
.nf

.B /dev/mouse
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Mouse
reports the pointer's position and buttons, one line per movement.
It is one of the files the
.B #c
device serves.
See
.IR cons (3)
for the rest of the directory.
.PP
A read parks until the mouse has moved, or a button has changed, since the
last line this file answered, and then answers exactly one line: the
letter
.BR m ,
followed by four eleven-character decimal fields, each followed by a
space:
.I x
and
.I y
in pixels, the button state as a bitmask with the 1, 2 and 4 bits for the
left, middle and right buttons, and a timestamp in milliseconds.
The line is always 49 bytes.
.PP
Nothing in the kernel reads this file first.
Unlike
.BR scancode " and " eia0
(see
.IR kbd (3)
and
.IR uart (3)),
opening
.B mouse
does not divert anything away from another consumer, because there is no
other consumer to divert it from.
.PP
.B Mouse
has one reader at a time.
An open while another fid already holds it fails with
.BR EBUSY .
An open on a machine with no mouse driver fails with
.BR ENXIO ,
so a program learns at once rather than parking on a file that will never
answer.
.PP
.B Mouse
is read-only.
A write fails with
.BR EPERM .
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/devfs/mouse.odin
.br
.B kernel/drivers/mouse
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR cons (3),
.IR kbd (3),
.IR uart (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Opening
.B mouse
while it is already held fails with
.BR EBUSY .
Opening it with no mouse present fails with
.BR ENXIO .
A write fails with
.BR EPERM .
A read that is still unanswered when the connection abandons it fails with
.BR EINTR .
